  - q: "Do I need to understand MCP to use AI at work?"
    a: "No. MCP is plumbing. What matters to you is the result — an assistant that can see your calendar or your document library instead of asking you to paste things in. It's worth knowing the word because it explains <em>why</em> some AI tools can reach your systems and others can't."
  - q: "Is MCP a Claude thing or an industry thing?"
    a: "Anthropic created MCP and released it as an open standard in late 2024. It has since been adopted well beyond Claude — the point of a standard is that it isn't owned by one product. You'll see it referred to across the AI tooling landscape."
  - q: "Is it safe to connect AI to our systems this way?"
    a: "It can be, and the control is in your hands: connections are granted per tool, and you decide what each one may reach. The risk isn't the protocol, it's granting broad access without thinking about it. Treat an MCP connection like giving a contractor a key — scope it to what they actually need."
  - q: "What's the difference between MCP and an API?"
    a: "An API is how two specific systems talk. MCP is a shared convention for how AI assistants talk to <em>any</em> tool, so the tool only has to be built once rather than separately for every assistant. In practice MCP servers often sit on top of existing APIs."
  - q: "Does this replace automations we already have?"
    a: "Not usually. Fixed automations are still the right answer for fixed, repetitive steps. MCP matters when you want an assistant to decide what to do and then actually do it — reading the real data rather than working from a description of it."
